   “I’ve been sending you reports for seven weeks. I thought you were
a department.”
   “I thought you were a department.”
   They stared at each other across a café table like two prisoners
who’d been tapping on the same wall.
   “What else are we?” said Vanessa, quietly.
   Sofía picked up her pen. “Departments,” she said. “That’s how they
stay out of rooms like this.”



The hearing was on a Thursday, at the community board, in the audit‐
orium of a middle school on Walton Avenue with a stage, a flag, and
chairs from three different decades.
   Before we went, Sofía laid down the rules in the café, at a volume
that carried to the sidewalk.
   “Nobody talks about stones. Nobody talks about visions. Nobody
says the word ‘frequency’ unless it’s a radio station. You talk about
exits. You talk about safety. You talk about a piece of paper with a date
on it.” She looked at me. “JC, you’re going to be very, very boring.”
   “I can be boring.”
   “You can’t. You’ve never once in your life been boring.” She sighed.
“Try.”
   The chair of the board was a retired MTA dispatcher named Mr.
Salcedo, who used his gavel like a metronome and had the unhurried
voice of a man who’d told thousands of people their train was delayed.
The agenda was posted on the wall. Item 4: Founders’ Night — Street
Closure and Amplified Sound Permit, Kingsbridge Armory. Public com‐
ment: two minutes per speaker. Sign-in sheet at the door.
   Mamá came, in her church coat, carrying a folding chair she’d
